perations from 125 to 375. Operation counts were identical (1000 per pool per period), and sync-north stayed at 2.5% in both periods, so the difference-in-differences is also +15.0 pp. The failed terminal attempts on sync-south moved from queue 5 ms / execution 245 ms to queue 235 ms / execution 15 ms, with queue plus execution equal to the 250 ms deadline in both cases; this points to requests exhausting their deadline while waiting for a worker rather than during execution. Deadline-policy change: not supported as the driver. The applied deadline was 250 ms on every operation in both periods on both pools, and no deadline_policy change was recorded for ledger-sync in 10:00–12:00, so the deadline is held constant. Worker/queue pressure: strongly supported by the pool-level telemetry (worker slots 16→4, queue depth 4→180) and by the shift of terminal-attempt time into queueing. Upstream p95 (118 ms) and CPU (0.42) are unchanged, which argues against an upstream slowdown or CPU saturation as the primary cause. Causal limitation: this is an observational before/after comparison with one treated pool and one control pool on a single day. The change timestamp coincides with the telemetry shift and the failure increase, but other concurrent factors not recorded here cannot be excluded, and the telemetry is sampled independently of operations, so the causal link is supported by temporal and mechanistic association, not proven."},"evidence_sql":["with ops as (select o.operation_id, o.route_id, o.opened_at_ms, case when o.opened_at_ms>=1778148000000 and o.opened_at_ms<1778151600000 then 'reference_10_11' when o.opened_at_ms>=1778151600000 and o.opened_at_ms<1778155200000 then 'incident_11_12' end period from logical_operations o join service_routes r using(route_id) join services s on s.service_id=r.service_id where s.service_code='ledger-sync' and o.opened_at_ms>=1778148000000 and o.opened_at_ms<1778155200000), asg as (select ops.*, ra.pool_id, ra.profile_id from ops join 